## Proof

We consider the point evaluation functional
$$A(g) = g(t_0) = \int_0^1 g(t)\,\delta(t-t_0)\,dt,$$
defined on the space $C[0,1]$ of continuous functions on $[0,1]$ equipped with the supremum norm
$$\|g\| = \max_{t\in[0,1]}|g(t)|.$$

The norm of the operator is
$$\|A\| = \sup_{g\neq 0}\frac{|Ag|}{\|g\|} = \sup_{g\neq 0}\frac{|g(t_0)|}{\|g\|}.$$

**Upper bound.** For every $g\in C[0,1]$ with $g\neq 0$,
$$|g(t_0)| \le \max_{t\in[0,1]}|g(t)| = \|g\|,$$
since $t_0\in[0,1]$. Therefore
$$\frac{|g(t_0)|}{\|g\|} \le 1 \quad\text{for all } g\neq 0,$$
which gives $\|A\| \le 1$.

**Lower bound (attainment).** Take the constant function $g(t)=1$ for all $t\in[0,1]$. Then $g\in C[0,1]$, $g\neq 0$, and
$$Ag = g(t_0) = 1, \qquad \|g\| = \max_{t\in[0,1]}|1| = 1.$$
Hence
$$\frac{|Ag|}{\|g\|} = \frac{1}{1} = 1.$$

Since the supremum is attained, we conclude
$$\|A\| = \sup_{g\neq 0}\frac{|g(t_0)|}{\|g\|} = 1.$$

**Remark on the role of the Dirac delta.** The representation $g(t_0)=\int_0^1 g(t)\delta(t-t_0)\,dt$ is a formal (distributional) expression of the sifting property of the Dirac delta. The delta "function" is not an element of $L^2[0,1]$ (it is not square-integrable), so on $L^2[0,1]$ the point evaluation functional is **unbounded** and its operator norm is infinite. The norm is finite and well-defined precisely on spaces of continuous functions such as $C[0,1]$ with the sup norm, where point evaluation is a bounded linear functional.

### Conclusion

$$\boxed{\|A\| = 1}$$

